  "slug": "archaea-duplication-d927",
  "summary": "GeneFamHunter is reported to outperform annotation-based methods for recovering divergent and tandemly arrayed gene-family members from assemblies.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that gene and protein inventories are increasingly derived from sequence evidence rather than from per-genome annotation. GeneFamHunter searches nucleotide assemblies directly with six-frame window searches to recover divergent and tandemly arrayed gene-family members, and is reported to outperform annotation-based methods. A proteogenomic study of Chlamydomonas nivalis combines transcriptome and related-species genome sORFs to discover small encoded peptides without the reference genome. A bacterial proteome analysis uses MMseqs2 clustering to define core protein sets and flags length outliers as likely artifacts of frameshifts. These items address different organisms and goals, so the shared element is evidence-based recovery of coding content beyond annotation, not a common method. None of the items demonstrates graph-based pangenome structural variant detection.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-07"
}
